---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { AFFILIATE } from "../lib/site.ts";
---

<Base
  title="Protect yourself"
  description="Five habits that limit the damage when a Japanese site you use is breached."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="prose page">
      <h1>Limit the damage from the next breach</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        You can't stop a company from being hacked. You can make sure a leak from one site doesn't open up the rest of
        your accounts. These five habits do most of the work.
      </p>

      <h2>Use a different email address for each site</h2>
      <p>
        Sign up with a separate alias for each shop, point card and app, all forwarding to your real inbox. When one site
        leaks, the leaked address can't be linked to your other accounts. You'll also know who leaked it when spam starts
        arriving at that alias, and you can switch the alias off.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={AFFILIATE.fastmail} rel="sponsored noopener">Fastmail</a> (paid) creates a masked address for
          each site from your inbox or password manager. <span class="disclosure">Affiliate link: we may earn a commission if
          you subscribe. It costs you nothing extra.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://duckduckgo.com/email/" rel="noopener">DuckDuckGo Email Protection</a> (free) forwards
          @duck.com aliases to the inbox you already have.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://relay.firefox.com/" rel="noopener">Firefox Relay</a> has a free plan with a small number of
          aliases.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Use a password manager and a unique password everywhere</h2>
      <p>
        Attackers try leaked passwords on other sites within hours. If every password is different, one leak stays one
        leak. A password manager such as <a href="https://bitwarden.com/" rel="noopener">Bitwarden</a> (free plan) creates
        and remembers them for you.
      </p>

      <h2>Turn on two-factor authentication</h2>
      <p>
        Use an authenticator app or a passkey where a site offers one. SMS codes are better than nothing but can be
        intercepted. Start with email, banking, and any shop that stores your card.
      </p>

      <h2>Delete accounts you no longer use</h2>
      <p>
        Old point-card, online shop and furusato nozei accounts still hold your name, address and phone number. If you
        haven't used one in a year, close it. Don't let small shops save your card; enter it each time or use a wallet
        such as Apple Pay or Google Pay.
      </p>

      <h2>Treat apology emails with suspicion</h2>
      <p>
        After a breach is announced, fake 「お詫び」 emails and SMS messages follow. They often offer compensation points
        or ask you to confirm your details. Go to the company's website yourself instead of clicking the link.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(var(--step-2), 4vw, var(--step-3));
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .disclosure {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
